// Module 04, Exercise 04: Self-Referencing Structs
//
// In C, a struct can contain a pointer to itself.
// This is how linked lists, trees, and other data structures work.
//
// In crunch-c, a pine field names the floorplan it points at — including
// its own, which is what makes a linked list possible.

// Define a linked list node.
floorplan Node {
    value: i32
    next: pine[Node]
}

// Create three nodes.
n1 = garrison(Node)
n1.value = i32(10)
n1.next = none        // no next node yet

n2 = garrison(Node)
n2.value = i32(20)
n2.next = none

n3 = garrison(Node)
n3.value = i32(30)
n3.next = none

// Link them: n1 -> n2 -> n3
n1.next = n2
n2.next = n3

// Traverse the list. A null pine reads back as none, which is how you know
// you have reached the end.
current = n1
while current != none {
    print(f"node value: {current.value}")
    current = current.next
}

scorch(n1)
scorch(n2)
scorch(n3)

// YOUR TASK:
// 1. Run this file. Does it print 10, 20, 30?
// 2. Add a fourth node with value 40.
// 3. Write a function that counts the nodes in the list.
// 4. What happens if you forget to link a node?

// BONUS: Use bathysphere to inspect the raw memory
// of a node. Can you spot the value and the pointer?
